  "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a card from a set of cards numbered from $1$ to $12,$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when throwing a standard die that is biased toward landing on $1.$\n\nIII. The color obtained when a ball from a bag containing $5$ balls with distinct colors is chosen randomly.",
  "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of cards, and since the selection is made at random, each card has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the die is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting $1.$\n\n- Random variable III can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
